{
  "abstract": "We study odd two-fan patches in local list-coloring arguments for the Earth–Moon problem. The graph $J(a,b)$ consists of adjacent hubs, two internally disjoint paths of lengths $a,b$ with common endpoints, and all edges from each hub to its corresponding path. For every odd $a,b\\ge3$, any two additional list slots, placed arbitrarily and possibly at one vertex, make the degree-minus-one lists colorable. Explicit graph-polynomial coefficients handle most placements; four first-order remainder identities settle the hub pair. For all $a,b\\in\\{3,5,7\\}$, one additional slot suffices. A finite proof uses only single-color linear and quadratic remainder equations, with 33 representative cases reduced to containment, omission, incidence, and twelve explicit coloring certificates. We also prove bare $J(5,5)$ degree-minus-one choosability by a new argument replacing a withdrawn proof: independent modular calculations retain 104 of 4,095 color supports, and all 5,695 pairwise-intersecting support multisets are explicitly colored. A valid disjoint-support merge lemma justifies this finite reduction. In a ten-critical graph, an induced odd fan whose ambient degrees are at most ten must have all degrees ten and rainbow exterior neighbor sets; bare $J(5,5)$ cannot occur under that premise. An augmented-patch extension further forces minimum degree six in the exterior of $J(3,3)$, $J(3,5)$, or $J(3,7)$. Conditional biplanar identifications can supply the required list surplus. Complete proofs and independent reproducible checks accompany these local results; no unavoidability theorem or improved global coloring bound is claimed.",
